{
  "requirement": "{cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.criterion_trait`\n\n`Criterion::source_node_id` identifies the criterion's\n{term}`requirements.sdk.graph.criteria.source_node`:\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait Criterion<D: Domain>: Send + Sized {\n    // ...\n\n    /// The node this question is about.\n    fn source_node_id(&self) -> NodeId<D>;\n\n    // ...\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject has one obligation. `Criterion::source_node_id` must return the node the criterion is evaluated on, which the definition calls the source node. Two marked tests cover it.\n\n- `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[criteria_keep_typed_answers_and_persist_only_when_requested]` builds a `Review` criterion whose `source_node_id` returns its `source` field, which `Review::new` sets to `intern_node(Node(1))`.\n  - After `verify`, it asserts `value.source_node_id()` equals `domain.cache().intern_node(Node(1))`.\n  - It saves the evidence and reloads it, then asserts `loaded.source_node_id()` equals `value.source_node_id()`.\n  - It does this for several criterion paths and output types.\n- `src/authoring/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[domain_persists_and_reuses_a_negative_validation_through_the_sdk]` tests the real SDK `Review` criterion. It asserts `review().source_node_id()` equals the interned requirement node. It then uses that node for evidence load, store and reuse.\n\nA wrong source node would fail these equality assertions. The requirement states nothing else to test, so I found no gap.",
